      },
      {
        "elements": [
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 4.415011037527593,
            "width": 37.98882681564246,
            "height": 8.830022075055187,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 2)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "55aaf4d3-f7e3-4a3e-9196-01a6f7474f94"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Reforms in Agriculture and Economy</strong><br>\nAs a landowner himself, Cato was deeply interested in agriculture and wrote 'De Agri Cultura' about farming practices. This work is one of the earliest surviving complete Latin prose texts. In it, Cato gives practical advice on various aspects of farm management, reflecting his belief in hard work and productivity as key virtues of Roman life.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Censorship and Moral Legislation</strong><br>\nCato was elected as Censor in 184 BCE, which was one of the highest and most respected positions in Roman society. As Censor, he had the power to oversee public morality and government finance. He used this position to enforce laws against luxury and corruption, reflecting his austere personal values and strict sense of virtue.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Cato's Oratory and Public Speaking</strong><br>\nCato was renowned for his skills in oratory and was a persuasive public speaker. His speeches often targeted his political opponents and were aimed at swaying public opinion. Despite the lack of surviving writings of his speeches, his reputation as an orator influenced many later Roman politicians and historians.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
