          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Opposition to Carthage</strong><br>\nOne of Cato's most persistent political stances was his opposition to Carthage. He famously ended every speech with the phrase \"Ceterum censeo Carthaginem esse delendam,\" which means \"Furthermore, I consider that Carthage must be destroyed.\" His insistence contributed to the sentiment leading to the Third Punic War.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Personal Life and Character</strong><br>\nCato was known for his strict personal discipline, which extended to his family life. He was said to have educated his son at home, to avoid exposure to the softening influence of public education. This personal narrative further cemented his image as a paragon of traditional Roman values and stern moral rectitude.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Legacy and Historical Impact</strong><br>\nCato the Elder's legacy is complex; he was revered for his commitment to Roman values but criticized for his inflexibility and harshness. His influence extended beyond his lifetime, affecting Roman politics, culture, and social norms. His life and work provide a lens through which the dynamics of the Roman Republic can be examined and understood.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
